WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.208
SANDEEP PARIKH: --for joining
us this morning, folks.

00:00:02.208 --> 00:00:04.140
So today, Rob and I
are going to talk about

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:07.470
how do you manage
configuration, policies,

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.480
and resources at scale?

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.420
My name is Sandeep Parikh,
I'm an Anthos advocate

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:13.988
for Google Cloud.

00:00:13.988 --> 00:00:15.280
ROB LEIDLE: And I'm Rob Leidle.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.140
I'm a software engineer
for Google Cloud.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.490
SANDEEP PARIKH: All right, so
let's start with configuration.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:24.400
So if you take a step
back and you sort of

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.060
look at the high level
view of the Anthos stack,

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.060
you start at the bottom.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.940
We start with Kubernetes, and
then you layer in service mesh

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:31.760
on top of that.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.760
And then we've got to
layer for policy controls,

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.300
which we'll get to shortly.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.390
And then you've got your
application services.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:39.335
And then on the left
side of the diagram,

00:00:39.335 --> 00:00:42.730
we talk about how you get
configuration or applications,

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:47.540
how you get software into that
environment, those two things.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.267
And this is the typical
sort of approach

00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:50.850
we're trying to guide
customers around

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.560
as they think about how to
setup, deploy, and operate

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:57.390
an Anthos environment.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.870
So to start, we're going to
focus on config management.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.110
How do you get
objects and resources

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:04.930
configured in your deployment?

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:08.200
Now how do you deliver
that configuration?

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.990
So typically, what we still
see on a pretty regular basis

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:14.980
is that a lot of teams are
still kubectl to apply changes

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:16.360
to their environments.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.115
They're still doing very
imperative operations.

00:01:19.115 --> 00:01:20.740
And this is really
challenging because,

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.800
as these clusters grow,
not only in number

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.760
but in scale and size--

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.140
we see a lot of large-scale
multi-tenant deployments--

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.990
it can become challenging
for operator teams

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:35.740
to continue to use kubectl
or imperative approaches

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.770
because Kubernetes itself
is a declarative system.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:41.350
You declare what you want the
state of the cluster to be,

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.360
and you push that configuration.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.370
But that doesn't really match
up to imperative operations.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.970
So how do you transition that
sort of imperative approach

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.430
to one that fits the Kubernetes
model better, that's more

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:52.480
declarative in nature?

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:58.490
And the first step is we take
out that imperative approach,

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:02.850
and we replace it with
a central repository

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.060
where you store that
configuration information.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.130
So we start with
a Git repo, which

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:10.250
is something almost every
technical practitioner is

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:11.300
familiar with.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.620
You start with Git.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.950
And inside of that Git repo, we
store all of the configuration

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.340
about your cluster,
how you configure

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:22.790
your namespaces, any additional
metadata, labels, annotations,

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.700
pods, services, secrets,
volumes, you name it.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.880
If it's a Kubernetes object,
it can fit in that config repo.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:31.830
And what we're doing
with that is we're

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:33.570
trying to create
this sort of approach

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:37.200
where the state of the cluster
is what exactly matches

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.310
the state of your repository.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.227
So if it's not in
the Git repo, it's

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:42.810
not reflected in
your cluster as well.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.880
Once you adopt
that approach, that

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.930
state that you're
trying to declare,

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:53.450
becomes-- that configuration,
it's effectively data.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:55.400
And we store it just
like we store code.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.280
And because we're using
something that's like Git,

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:01.850
it becomes a process that
you can use typical software

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:05.240
development workflows to manage
your configuration manager

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:06.570
environments.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:11.030
So the process of deploying
a new config in production

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:12.950
follows the same software
development process

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:14.780
that you're already
familiar with, right?

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:18.500
If a team needs to create
a new configuration,

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.020
they can branch the repository,
they can make their changes,

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:22.940
they can validate them
locally, and then they

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:24.920
can have them merge back in.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.090
And then those changes get
pushed out to production.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:29.860
And as I mentioned,
at any given time,

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:32.460
we want to make sure the
state of your deployments

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.920
matches exactly what's
specified in that Git repo.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:41.410
And so that's where Anthos
Config Management comes in.

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:43.700
So with ACM, what
we're trying to give

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:47.870
you is the ability to define
the state of your deployment

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:51.500
and store that in a central,
secure Git repository.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.690
The benefits of doing
that are the same benefits

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.000
that apply to typical
software development, right?

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.880
We have the ability to audit
changes a lot more cleanly now.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.460
Instead of digging through
Kubernetes logs and events

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:03.410
and trying to figure
out who made a change

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:05.990
and what change they introduced
that might have broken

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:09.000
your infrastructure, we can just
trace that back to a set of Git

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.250
commits and who authored the
commit and maybe even who

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.430
merged it in.

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:15.560
You can revert your deployment
and roll things back

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:19.399
a lot easier just by making
a change in your Git config.

00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:22.700
You may revert a commit, and
now production can pick up

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:26.260
that revert they needed to do.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:29.240
ACM also is self-healing.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:33.440
So it watches the cluster for
drift from the configuration.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:36.370
So if, by chance, someone
goes and applies an imperative

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:39.520
operation and changes a
namespace configuration

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:44.568
outside of the normal workflow,
the mechanism built into ACM

00:04:44.568 --> 00:04:46.610
will actually sink that
change back in and put it

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:49.460
back to the state that's, again,
reflected in the repository.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.508
So at any given time, again,
the state of your cluster

00:04:52.508 --> 00:04:54.800
and the state of your deployment
matches exactly what's

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:56.908
in source control.

00:04:56.908 --> 00:04:58.450
And ultimately, the
best part of this

00:04:58.450 --> 00:05:01.480
is that you can version your
deployments just like you

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:02.710
version software.

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:05.050
So you can tag them, mark
them as release candidates,

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:07.900
et cetera, just like you would
any of your applications.

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:10.113
So it's a pretty
powerful thought process

00:05:10.113 --> 00:05:10.780
to work through.

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:15.700
Now, the way Anthos
Config Management, or ACM,

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:19.410
works in your environment from
an architectural perspective--

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.357
and you can see, in the
middle of the diagram,

00:05:21.357 --> 00:05:22.690
there's the interconnect, right?

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:25.360
That's bridging your on-prem
environment and your cloud

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:26.380
environment.

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:29.723
And this would be true for a
multi-cloud scenario as well.

00:05:29.723 --> 00:05:31.390
But what's happening
here is that you've

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:34.150
got the interconnect bridging
the networking between the two.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:37.120
And then, in each Kubernetes
environment or each GKE

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:40.810
environment, there's a
config management controller

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:42.400
that we deploy as an operator.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.650
And that controller is
what's keeping things in sync

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:45.770
with the repo.

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:49.090
Now, the best part is that repo
does not have to live on cloud.

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:52.287
That can live in your
on-prem infrastructure.

00:05:52.287 --> 00:05:53.870
So if you want to
make sure-- if there

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:57.320
are particular security or
compliance or guidelines

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:59.750
around where your
configuration lives,

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:01.400
where the definition
of that lives,

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:04.130
you can keep that on-prem
and then have the config just

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:07.145
spread out across all
of your GKE clusters.

00:06:11.457 --> 00:06:13.790
So that's a little bit about
how we manage configuration

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:15.260
at scale, right?

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:18.694
And let me go back for
a quick second here.

00:06:18.694 --> 00:06:23.450
So the way you configure
ACM is, you effectively,

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:26.073
when you deploy the software
inside of your Kubernetes

00:06:26.073 --> 00:06:27.740
environment, all you
really have to tell

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:30.770
it is, where does
that Git repo live,

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:33.140
what is the branch
or the tag you want

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:36.050
to use for your releases,
and any authentication

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:38.542
information it needs too
to access that Git repo.

00:06:38.542 --> 00:06:40.500
And once you've done
that, it's up and running.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:44.150
And now your clusters are
under that managed state

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:45.477
that ACM provides.

00:06:45.477 --> 00:06:47.060
So that's the config
management piece.

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:52.640
Now, how do we use
config management

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.080
to implement policy controls?

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:58.640
So the sort of act of creating
policies and enforcing them

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:02.790
is something we often
call policy management.

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:04.500
Think of policies
and policy management

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:06.270
as effectively
saying, I have a set

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.280
of rules, a set of
governance I want

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.230
to apply to all of
my environments,

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:11.850
to all of my deployment.

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:14.610
And I want to enforce those
rules in certain ways.

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:18.480
Either I want to not allow
objects that break those rules.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:20.890
Or I want to allow objects
that may violate those rules,

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:22.765
but I want to be able
to audit them and audit

00:07:22.765 --> 00:07:24.660
any of those violations
on an ongoing basis.

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:31.777
Now policy management
and config management,

00:07:31.777 --> 00:07:33.110
there's a little bit of overlap.

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:34.985
And I think, from a
mental model perspective,

00:07:34.985 --> 00:07:37.978
there's sometimes a
little bit of collapsing

00:07:37.978 --> 00:07:38.770
these two together.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:41.350
But they are very distinct,
especially in the way

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:43.040
that ACM sees them.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:46.650
So from a config
management perspective,

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:48.400
config management is
really all about how

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.040
do you store, control,
and govern your deployment

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:52.210
configuration.

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:54.790
There is typically a process
for how it gets done.

00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:57.755
In the case of ACM, that's
a GitOps-style process.

00:07:57.755 --> 00:07:59.380
And there's typically
a process for who

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:02.230
can do it using something like
Kubernetes role-based access

00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:04.550
control, or RBAC,
to control that.

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:06.220
But ultimately,
config management

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:10.930
is really about how you
enforce object or resource

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:13.060
state in the cluster.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:16.300
On the other hand, policy
management is about rules.

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:19.240
It's about setting guardrails
and applying governance.

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:22.900
And it's really more about,
can you make a change

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:23.990
to a given object?

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:25.970
Is that change allowed?

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.030
And how do we want to
enforce those changes?

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:29.890
Do we want to, again,
deny them entirely

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:32.140
or allow those changes
to happen and then

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:34.615
audit them for kind
of ongoing violations?

00:08:37.294 --> 00:08:39.419
A lot of times, I think
the policy management angle

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:41.789
can actually sound quite
a bit like Kubernetes

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:43.355
RBAC in many ways.

00:08:43.355 --> 00:08:44.730
But again, if you
think about it,

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:46.830
RBAC is about who
can make a change

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:48.810
and who has the power
to make a change.

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:51.300
Whereas, the policy
aspect is really about,

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:52.572
is that change even allowed?

00:08:52.572 --> 00:08:55.030
So it's a little bit of a higher
level view of the problem.

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:00.720
Now, I mentioned
guardrails and governance.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:04.600
Kubernetes is an incredibly
powerful distributed system,

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:05.210
right?

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:07.100
And powerful systems
like that typically

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:09.590
need some kind of control
because you want to watch out

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:11.780
for potential problem spots.

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:14.840
You want to make sure you
haven't given a particular set

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:16.702
of users too many privileges.

00:09:16.702 --> 00:09:19.160
You don't want to give them
too much power to make changes.

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:21.583
You want to make sure that
services are locked down

00:09:21.583 --> 00:09:23.000
and that they can
only be accessed

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:26.180
by other services
you deem appropriate

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:28.620
or by certain parties
you deem appropriate.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:30.120
And obviously, you
want to watch out

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:31.770
for things like exfiltration.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:34.050
You don't want data
leaving your environments

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:36.600
in an unintended fashion.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:38.460
But on the other
hand, those controls

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:40.260
that you want to
use, that governance,

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:42.700
needs to be agile and flexible.

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.750
You want to be able to
scope that governance

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:48.450
and that control very narrowly
to only the objects you really

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:49.940
care about.

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:52.400
And you want those
policies and those controls

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.650
to be written by
the people who know

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:57.710
the compliance, the regulatory
work, your organization's

00:09:57.710 --> 00:09:58.490
policies best.

00:09:58.490 --> 00:10:01.353
So you've got to make it easy
to develop and deploy them.

00:10:01.353 --> 00:10:03.770
Because at the end of the day,
what we're trying to get to

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:05.330
is safe deployments.

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:07.430
We don't want
anything in production

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:09.140
that could violate
any of the rules

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:11.000
we need to worry about
on a regular basis.

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:18.510
So as part of ACM, we
have another component

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:21.630
wrapped in there called
ACM Policy Controller.

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:24.630
Policy Controller is
based on a project

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:27.660
from Open Policy
Agent and Gatekeeper.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.570
Gatekeeper is an offshoot
of Open Policy Agent.

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:33.960
And Open Policy Agent is really
just a general purpose policy

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:35.010
engine.

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:37.510
You write a policy,
you hand it some data,

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:39.930
it makes a decision based
on those two things.

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:41.940
Does that data
violate the policy,

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:44.040
or does it not
violate the policy?

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:46.800
And what Gatekeeper and,
by extension, ACM Policy

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:49.950
Controller do is it
takes that policy engine,

00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:52.530
and it makes it a first-class
citizen in your Kubernetes

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:53.430
environment.

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:56.490
And it makes it into an
admission controller.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:59.710
So now that means
the policy mechanism

00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:04.400
is right alongside the
ability to admit objects

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:06.180
to the cluster.

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.745
So now you can apply
rules upon admission.

00:11:08.745 --> 00:11:10.370
And so something that
could potentially

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:13.460
violate the rules that your
organization has won't get

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:15.260
admitted to the cluster.

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:17.110
Or again, you could say
things like, well, I

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:18.620
want to allow this
to happen, but I

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:21.720
want to audit anyone who's
potentially out of compliance.

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:23.935
It has that sort of capability.

00:11:23.935 --> 00:11:25.310
But because it
integrates, again,

00:11:25.310 --> 00:11:27.240
as a first-class
citizen with Kubernetes,

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:30.440
and with GKE by extension,
all of the standard Kubernetes

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:33.760
tooling you're used to, it
fits right alongside with it,

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:37.010
can be used alongside with it.

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:39.680
So let's talk about
a policy in depth

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:41.520
and what the
structure looks like.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:44.780
So there are two parts
to a policy, right?

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:47.420
There is the rule
definition, which

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:49.250
is written in a
language called Rego,

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:52.700
which is part of the OPA
package, the Open Policy Agent

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:53.480
package.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:56.240
And then there's the actual
packaging of that rule

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:57.950
and giving it to Kubernetes.

00:11:57.950 --> 00:12:00.860
And so that packaging is
called a ConstraintTemplate.

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:02.340
And it's a parametrized piece.

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:03.890
You can apply arguments.

00:12:03.890 --> 00:12:05.340
You can scope it down.

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:08.580
But ultimately,
the policy approach

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:10.410
is built into two components.

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:12.540
One is the rule definition.

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:15.420
And the second part is how
that rule gets enforced.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:17.690
So the ConstraintTemplate
here that you see

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:19.280
is the rule definition.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:20.810
What are we trying to enforce?

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:23.090
What are we trying to evaluate?

00:12:23.090 --> 00:12:26.105
How many of you are familiar
with Istio in the room?

00:12:26.105 --> 00:12:27.070
All right, a handful.

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:29.250
So you'll hear more
about Istio later today.

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:32.130
But one of the most
powerful aspects of Istio

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:35.970
is that you can automatically
enable things like MTLS

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:38.010
to mutually
authenticate services

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:40.030
and clients in your cluster.

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:41.910
But one of your
organization's policies

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:43.530
could be, hey, we
want to make sure

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:45.180
that every service
that gets deployed,

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:49.970
every client in our environment,
must support mutual TLS.

00:12:49.970 --> 00:12:52.560
And you want to also make sure
that no one turns that off,

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:52.760
right?

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:54.230
You don't want anyone
to bypass that,

00:12:54.230 --> 00:12:55.522
because that could be possible.

00:12:55.522 --> 00:12:58.320
Someone who has the appropriate
RBAC permissions could say,

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:00.373
well, my service is special.

00:13:00.373 --> 00:13:01.790
I want to turn off
MTLS because we

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:03.570
don't want to support it today.

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:05.060
So what this rule
is actually doing

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:07.770
is it's evaluating part of
that process and saying,

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:11.360
hey, if you try to turn off
MTLS, I'm going to catch that,

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:12.990
and that's going to
throw a violation.

00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:15.930
So this is the rule
that catches that.

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:18.377
And then the enforcement
half is packaged in something

00:13:18.377 --> 00:13:19.210
called a constraint.

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:21.740
And let me jump
back a quick second.

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:23.440
But what we're
doing in this policy

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:26.350
and this ConstraintTemplate is,
if you look at that kind field

00:13:26.350 --> 00:13:28.000
about a third of
the way down, we're

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:31.963
actually defining a new custom
resource for Kubernetes.

00:13:31.963 --> 00:13:33.880
We're saying, hey, there
is now a new resource

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:36.790
called,
destinationruletlsenabled,

00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:39.470
that lives in the
Kubernetes environment.

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:42.280
And if you actually
run kubectl get CRDs,

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:45.080
you'll see that on the list.

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:47.050
So what the constraint
does is it actually

00:13:47.050 --> 00:13:51.970
takes that, CR and it creates
another spec based around that.

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:54.028
And it lets us scope
the enforcement.

00:13:54.028 --> 00:13:56.570
And so there are two parts of
this that are really important.

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:59.590
One is enforcement action
about a third of the way down.

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:02.150
In this example, I've set
this constraint to be denied.

00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:05.070
So if anybody tries to submit
a destination rule that

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:08.380
deactivates MTLS, that thing
will get denied admission

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:09.380
to the cluster entirely.

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:11.420
It won't even get in.

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:13.360
And the second part of
it is, about halfway

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:19.330
down, where I'm actually scoping
that to a particular API object

00:14:19.330 --> 00:14:21.230
and to a particular namespace.

00:14:21.230 --> 00:14:23.380
So I'm saying, only
scope this rule

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:27.550
to destination rule objects
that are part of the Istio API,

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:31.300
and only look for them
in the default namespace.

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:33.577
Now, the nice thing is,
with ACM Policy Controller,

00:14:33.577 --> 00:14:35.660
you can actually adjust
that scoping a little bit.

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:39.090
You can say, I want to have
it apply to any namespace

00:14:39.090 --> 00:14:41.040
that has the following
labels or that doesn't

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:42.300
have the following labels.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:44.940
So you can actually control
the enforcement and application

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:46.260
of that constraint as well.

00:14:49.302 --> 00:14:50.760
And so now, what
would happen if we

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:52.560
were to push that
ConstraintTemplate

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:54.540
and that corresponding
constraint,

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:58.760
if I try to push this new
destination rule that disables

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:02.850
MTLS, GKE will
accept the object.

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:05.930
The admission controller will
then review it, and then decide

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:08.422
and see that it violates
the rule we've laid out

00:15:08.422 --> 00:15:10.130
and then just kick it
right back and say,

00:15:10.130 --> 00:15:12.118
sorry, we can't take it.

00:15:12.118 --> 00:15:14.160
And you can supply your
own custom error messages

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:15.507
and things like that.

00:15:15.507 --> 00:15:17.840
But it will automatically
just push that object back out

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:19.850
and say, I can't accept
this, because, again, it

00:15:19.850 --> 00:15:20.690
violates a rule.

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:27.260
So now, there are three stages
to the policy development

00:15:27.260 --> 00:15:27.968
process.

00:15:27.968 --> 00:15:29.510
There's obviously
the authoring step,

00:15:29.510 --> 00:15:31.135
which is where you're
going to validate

00:15:31.135 --> 00:15:33.567
the rules in your approach.

00:15:33.567 --> 00:15:35.150
And then there's the
application of it

00:15:35.150 --> 00:15:37.580
where you may want
to just firmly deny

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:39.290
anything that is
out of compliance

00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:41.400
or that violates your rules.

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:42.810
Or there's a third
step where you

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:46.350
can say we want to allow
objects in that may potentially

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:49.260
have violations, and we
just want to audit them.

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:52.560
And there are
things where you may

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:55.730
want audit controls for
maybe labels or annotations.

00:15:55.730 --> 00:15:57.540
But for something that's
violating-- again,

00:15:57.540 --> 00:16:01.980
regulatory compliance or any
kind of corporate IT policy--

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:03.750
those may be objects
where you want

00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:05.460
to deny admission entirely.

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:09.180
Again, we want to watch out for
things like data exfiltration

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:10.335
or overexposed services.

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:16.520
So some of the example policy
use cases-- and by the way,

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:18.770
I should mention there are
actually a whole bunch that

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:21.070
are already included with
ACM Policy Controller, things

00:16:21.070 --> 00:16:23.710
like checking for
required labels

00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:26.860
or particular annotations
or a particular structure.

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:29.980
You can do things with
policies like make sure

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:32.260
that resource constraints
are baked in, right?

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:34.330
You could say, hey,
no pods and services

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:37.360
can be deployed in this cluster
unless they actually call out

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:40.270
how much CPU and RAM they
need so that we can make

00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:43.150
sure that we're not overdoing
the amount of resources we're

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:45.370
going to consume for a
given pod or service.

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:49.250
But particularly around
security concerns,

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:52.810
we can watch out for things
like only allowing access

00:16:52.810 --> 00:16:56.350
from certain domains within
your organization, right?

00:16:56.350 --> 00:16:59.080
So a service within a particular
domain can access another one,

00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:01.300
but one outside of
that list of domains

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:04.520
may not be able to
access services.

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:06.420
Like in the example
I showed earlier,

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:08.910
we can require strict
MTLS across the board

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:10.609
and make sure that
stays enforced

00:17:10.609 --> 00:17:14.599
so that no one can turn off
that authentication piece.

00:17:14.599 --> 00:17:17.210
You may want to implement
controls like service A can

00:17:17.210 --> 00:17:20.089
talk to a service B, but
service A definitely cannot talk

00:17:20.089 --> 00:17:22.530
to service C, and
make that a policy.

00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:26.130
So again, if anyone tries to
bypass that or override it,

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:27.380
they will actually get booted.

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:30.263
That configuration won't be
allowed into their cluster.

00:17:30.263 --> 00:17:31.680
And there are many
other examples.

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:32.958
These are just a few.

00:17:32.958 --> 00:17:35.250
And we're happy to kind of
answer questions around them

00:17:35.250 --> 00:17:39.595
as we have time to afterwards.

00:17:39.595 --> 00:17:41.470
So with that, I'm going
to hand it off to Rob

00:17:41.470 --> 00:17:43.777
to talk about managing
platform resources.

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:49.150
ROB LEIDLE: Yeah,
so thanks Sandeep.

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:52.090
Kind of bringing
this together, how

00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:54.980
could you apply
the same techniques

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:58.118
Sandeep talked about to
your cloud infrastructure?

00:18:01.540 --> 00:18:02.900
That's not possible today.

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:05.560
You're going to have some
sort of split-brain approach.

00:18:05.560 --> 00:18:08.890
You're going to have
Kubernetes applications managed

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:11.103
through your
Kubernetes tool chain.

00:18:11.103 --> 00:18:13.520
And then you're going to have
your cloud resources managed

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:17.750
through some other mechanism,
be it cloud or Develop

00:18:17.750 --> 00:18:19.090
Manager, et cetera.

00:18:23.380 --> 00:18:28.080
We have now made available a new
product, GCP Config Connector.

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:30.910
And this allows you to manage
your cloud infrastructure

00:18:30.910 --> 00:18:33.210
in a Kubernetes native way.

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:35.860
And since it is
Kubernetes native,

00:18:35.860 --> 00:18:41.580
integrations like Anthos Config
Management just work with it.

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:47.970
The idea here is that
kubectl, Config Management,

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:48.870
or API clients--

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:50.770
I guess kubectl
is an API client--

00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:53.730
but API clients like
your CI/CD pipeline.

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:57.450
And they can push configuration
to the Kubernetes API server.

00:18:57.450 --> 00:19:00.570
And then GCP Config
Connector will

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:02.670
take that declared
configuration,

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:08.080
your intended state, and it
will make it a reality in GCP.

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:10.970
So Config Connector, like I
said, is Kubernetes native.

00:19:10.970 --> 00:19:13.760
The actual configuration
is the care

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:16.710
you've come to expect
from Kubernetes.

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:21.736
You can use the K8s APIs or
kubectl to manipulate it.

00:19:21.736 --> 00:19:25.790
It allows you to declare
your intended state

00:19:25.790 --> 00:19:27.288
with declarative configuration.

00:19:27.288 --> 00:19:28.830
So you avoid a lot
of the issues that

00:19:28.830 --> 00:19:32.590
come from imperative workflows.

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:34.640
It is eventually consistent.

00:19:34.640 --> 00:19:37.285
So you can apply
your configuration

00:19:37.285 --> 00:19:39.160
in any order you want,
and it will eventually

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:41.320
reach the state you intend.

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:44.170
That also means that it'll be
quicker and more performant.

00:19:44.170 --> 00:19:45.130
It's item potent.

00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:47.213
You can apply the same
configuration over and over

00:19:47.213 --> 00:19:49.050
again, and you'll see no change.

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:50.130
And it combats drift.

00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:54.190
So you can ensure that changes
made by operators outside

00:19:54.190 --> 00:19:58.930
of your application's CI/CD
push flow are reverted back.

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:01.030
So you don't end
up in a state where

00:20:01.030 --> 00:20:04.450
there are changes that are
not in your repository.

00:20:04.450 --> 00:20:06.880
It's available through
Anthos as a GKE

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:10.010
add-on or manual installation.

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:12.895
And it's in beta now
and going GA in Q1.

00:20:15.590 --> 00:20:20.800
So here's a little talk
about the actual KRM.

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:23.530
On the right, we
have two examples.

00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:26.010
We have a Pub/Sub topic--

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:28.995
which Pub/Sub is a
popular GCP service.

00:20:28.995 --> 00:20:32.510
And topic is a resource
within Pub/Sub.

00:20:32.510 --> 00:20:34.390
And then we have a
Pub/Sub subscription.

00:20:34.390 --> 00:20:37.920
A subscription is
associated with a topic.

00:20:37.920 --> 00:20:39.930
That's just the way
subscriptions work.

00:20:39.930 --> 00:20:44.340
And in this example, you can
see in the subscriptions spec,

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:47.110
we have a field called topicRef.

00:20:47.110 --> 00:20:49.842
And in topicRef, we say
the name is my-topic.

00:20:49.842 --> 00:20:51.300
And that's saying
this subscription

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:55.150
is associated with that topic.

00:20:55.150 --> 00:21:00.700
And that's just an example of
how these configurations work

00:21:00.700 --> 00:21:04.990
in the way you would expect a
Kubernetes resource to work.

00:21:04.990 --> 00:21:07.420
And in terms of what
resources are supported,

00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:10.470
we support resources from all
the services listed there,

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:13.980
notably Compute,
Spanner, Storage.

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:18.520
and resource coverage
is expanding.

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:20.380
So putting it all
together, you can

00:21:20.380 --> 00:21:23.260
use your Anthos configuration
and policy management

00:21:23.260 --> 00:21:25.600
to manage your GCP
infrastructure as well.

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:28.510
You can bring all those
techniques to GCP.

00:21:28.510 --> 00:21:32.320
In addition, you can
standardize your tool chain

00:21:32.320 --> 00:21:38.860
on Kubernetes tools,
which will simplify

00:21:38.860 --> 00:21:42.960
your environment for developers
and make them more productive.

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:45.520
That'll give you back more of
your most limited resource,

00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:46.630
which is developer time.

00:21:46.630 --> 00:21:49.090
And you'll have more time
to work on your applications

00:21:49.090 --> 00:21:51.730
and things that matter
to your business.

00:21:51.730 --> 00:21:54.430
And that's it for policy
configuration management.

00:21:54.430 --> 00:21:55.930
Sandeep and I and
other Googlers are

00:21:55.930 --> 00:21:59.080
available to answer any
questions as needed.

00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:01.180
Thank you.

00:22:01.180 --> 00:22:03.280
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:05.380 --> 00:22:08.130
SANDEEP PARIKH: [INAUDIBLE]

